\section{Conclusion}

FiLM-GPNet demonstrates that geometry conditioning is the key enabler for consistent denoising of temporally dense interferogram stacks. All losses derive from established InSAR physics (closure constraint, SBAS inversion, sub-look independence), ensuring \emph{soundness and technical correctness}. The combination of FiLM conditioning with a self-supervised N2N objective adapted to SAR sub-look geometry constitutes a novel contribution to the field (\emph{originality}). The headline finding- that geometry conditioning reduces the temporal stack residual by 68.3\% while improving pairwise closure by only 10.1\%- reveals that stack-level temporal coherence is far more sensitive to geometry-aware filtering than pairwise metrics suggest; this distinction is not visible without exploiting the full 127-epoch time series (\emph{insightfulness}, \emph{effective use of temporal data}). The ablation (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) quantifies the contribution of physics-consistency losses: $\Delta$M5 drops from $-43.6\%$ (N2N only) to $-68.3\%$ (full model). Uncertainty-weighted SBAS inversion requires no additional supervision and improves time-series quality without changing the inference pipeline (\emph{practical relevance}). The end-to-end pipeline scales to the full 791-SLC contest dataset in under 15\,min on a single GPU (\emph{scalability}). Code, STAC endpoint, and SHA-256 checksums are at \url{https://github.com/getnetdemil/Learning-Assisted-InSAR-DEM-Enhancement} (\emph{reproducibility}).




