\begin{abstract}
Dense temporal stacks of commercial X-band SAR imagery enable fine-grained surface-deformation monitoring, but phase quality degrades sharply when acquisitions span wide ranges of incidence angles and orbital geometries. Classical Goldstein filtering is geometry-blind, producing inconsistent interferograms and elevated triplet-closure errors across heterogeneous stacks. We propose \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}, a FiLM-conditioned U-Net that restores wrapped complex interferograms by conditioning on a seven-dimensional per-pair geometry vector $[\Delta t,\,\theta_{\rm inc},\,\theta_{\rm graze},\,B_\perp,\,{\rm mode},\,{\rm look},\,{\rm SNR}]$. Training is pseudo-supervised: Goldstein-filtered interferograms serve as pseudo-targets and are augmented with triplet-closure and SBAS temporal residual losses—requiring no clean reference interferograms. The model additionally predicts per-pixel aleatoric uncertainty, propagated downstream as SBAS inversion weights. Applied to the IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest Hawaii stack, FiLM-GPNet substantially outperforms Goldstein in temporal consistency and DEM quality, and generalizes \emph{zero-shot} to a geographically distinct site without retraining. Code and data are publicly available.

Github Repo: \href{https://github.com/getnetdemil/Learning-Assisted-InSAR-DEM-Enhancement}{https://github.com/getnetdemil/Learning-Assisted-InSAR-DEM-Enhancement}
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
InSAR, phase denoising, self-supervised learning, Noise2Noise,
FiLM conditioning, SBAS, Capella Space, phase closure
\end{IEEEkeywords}

