\section{Introduction}

The rapid growth of commercial small-satellite SAR constellations has increased the availability of high-resolution SAR observations for interferometric analysis~\cite{11314025}. However, dense temporal stacks also expose a key limitation of classical InSAR processing: coherence and phase quality deteriorate when acquisitions differ substantially in temporal and perpendicular baseline, incidence angle, and viewing geometry~\cite{11186241}. This is especially relevant for Capella Spotlight data, where repeated observations of the same area can vary substantially across time. Classical filters such as Goldstein and boxcar filtering are geometry-blind, applying essentially the same smoothing strategy to all interferograms regardless of whether a pair is short-baseline and highly coherent or long-baseline and strongly decorrelated~\cite{goldstein1998interferometric}. The result is noisier interferograms, weaker temporal consistency, larger closure errors, more difficult phase unwrapping, and less reliable downstream inference~\cite{massonnet1998radar,zebker1992decorrelation}. While deep learning offers an alternative, supervised phase restoration remains limited by the lack of clean reference interferograms in real InSAR stacks.


To address this limitation, we propose \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}, a geometry-aware pseudo-supervised framework for phase restoration in large temporal InSAR stacks. FiLM-GPNet operates on wrapped complex interferograms while conditioning each pair on acquisition geometry, allowing the restoration process to adapt to temporal separation, incidence angle, perpendicular baseline, and viewing configuration. Rather than using clean reference labels, the model is trained with Goldstein-filtered interferograms as pseudo-targets, enabling it to learn a geometry-conditioned generalization of a fixed classical filter across heterogeneous acquisition conditions. In addition to restoring phase, FiLM-GPNet predicts aleatoric uncertainty, which is retained as an auxiliary confidence layer for downstream unwrapping and SBAS-compatible processing. This formulation is intended to improve temporal consistency across diverse stacks while preserving transferability across acquisition settings. In this work, we evaluate FiLM-GPNet against a classical InSAR baseline on Capella Spotlight data and show that geometry-conditioned restoration achieves robust zero-shot generalization across distinct stacks without retraining.
