To address this limitation, we propose \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}, a geometry-aware pseudo-supervised framework for phase restoration in large temporal InSAR stacks. FiLM-GPNet operates on wrapped complex interferograms while conditioning each pair on acquisition geometry, allowing the restoration process to adapt to temporal separation, incidence angle, perpendicular baseline, and viewing configuration. Rather than using clean reference labels, the model is trained with Goldstein-filtered interferograms as pseudo-targets, enabling it to learn a geometry-conditioned generalization of a fixed classical filter across heterogeneous acquisition conditions. In addition to restoring phase, FiLM-GPNet predicts aleatoric uncertainty, which is retained as an auxiliary confidence layer for downstream unwrapping and SBAS-compatible processing. This formulation is intended to improve temporal consistency across diverse stacks while preserving transferability across acquisition settings. In this work, we evaluate FiLM-GPNet against a classical InSAR baseline on Capella Spotlight data and show that geometry-conditioned restoration achieves robust zero-shot generalization across distinct stacks without retraining.

