\section{Introduction}

The rapid growth of commercial small-satellite SAR constellations has increased the availability of high-resolution SAR observations for interferometric analysis~\cite{11314025}. However, dense temporal stacks also expose a key limitation of classical InSAR processing: coherence and phase quality deteriorate when acquisitions differ substantially in temporal and perpendicular baseline, incidence angle, and viewing geometry~\cite{11186241}. This is especially relevant for Capella Spotlight data, where repeated observations of the same area can vary substantially across time. Classical filters such as Goldstein and boxcar filtering are geometry-blind, applying essentially the same smoothing strategy to all interferograms regardless of whether a pair is short-baseline and highly coherent or long-baseline and strongly decorrelated~\cite{goldstein1998interferometric}. The result is noisier interferograms, weaker temporal consistency, larger closure errors, more difficult phase unwrapping, and less reliable downstream inference~\cite{massonnet1998radar,zebker1992decorrelation}. While deep learning offers an alternative, supervised phase restoration remains limited by the lack of clean reference interferograms in real InSAR stacks.


To address this limitation, we propose \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}, a geometry-aware pseudo-supervised framework for phase restoration in large temporal InSAR stacks. FiLM-GPNet operates on wrapped complex interferograms while conditioning each pair on acquisition geometry, allowing the restoration process to adapt to temporal separation, incidence angle, perpendicular baseline, and viewing configuration. Rather than using clean reference labels, the model is trained with Goldstein-filtered interferograms as pseudo-targets, enabling it to learn a geometry-conditioned generalization of a fixed classical filter across heterogeneous acquisition conditions. In addition to restoring phase, FiLM-GPNet predicts aleatoric uncertainty, which is retained as an auxiliary confidence layer for downstream unwrapping and SBAS-compatible processing. This formulation is intended to improve temporal consistency across diverse stacks while preserving transferability across acquisition settings. In this work, we evaluate FiLM-GPNet against a classical InSAR baseline on Capella Spotlight data and show that geometry-conditioned restoration achieves robust zero-shot generalization across distinct stacks without retraining.
% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Method}
Figure 1 in the appendix provides an overview of the various steps involved in the overall data processing pipeline. These are discussed individually in the following sub-sections.

\subsection{Geometry-Aware Pair-Graph Construction}

\begin{figure*}[t!]
  \centering
    \includegraphics[width=16cm]{Figures/FiLMUNet-Full.png}
    \caption{The FiLM-GPNet architecture. It comprises  input/output blocks, FiLM conditioning-based encoder/decoder network and MLP for processing of geometry feature vector.}
    \label{FiLMUNet-Full}
\end{figure*}


Given a manifest of $N$ raw SLC acquisitions within each spatial group, we construct a strict interferometric pair graph that retains only geometrically compatible pairs for InSAR processing. An edge $(i,j)$ is accepted only if the two acquisitions belong to the same AOI, share orbit state, look direction, orbital plane, platform, and instrument mode, and satisfy temporal and geometric consistency constraints. In our experiments, we require $\Delta t \leq 30$ days and $|\Delta \theta_{\mathrm{inc}}| \leq 2^\circ$, while additionally enforcing bounded look-angle and squint-angle differences when available. We also reject pairs with excessive range or azimuth sampling mismatch and require sufficient footprint overlap through a lightweight bounding-box test. This conservative filtering removes weak or physically inconsistent pairs before coregistration and interferogram formation. The surviving edges are ranked by a soft quality score $Q_{ij}$ as defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:qscore}
\begin{equation}
Q_{ij}
=
\frac{1}{1+\Delta t_{ij}}
\cdot
\frac{1}{1+|\Delta \theta_{\mathrm{inc},ij}|}
\cdot
G_{ij},
\label{eq:qscore}
\end{equation}
where $G_{ij}$ is an additional geometry-consistency factor that penalizes look-angle mismatch, squint mismatch, range/azimuth sampling mismatch, and low footprint overlap whenever those metadata are available. Closure triplets are then enumerated from the accepted graph for later physics-based supervision.

\subsection{PFA-Aware Coregistration and Classical Baseline}

Because the Capella Spotlight PFA SLC pairs used in this work can exhibit geometry-dependent misregistration and variable interferometric quality, we apply a compact engineering pipeline for precise pairwise alignment before learning-based phase restoration. For each accepted pair, a metadata-derived scene-reference seed is refined on multilooked log-amplitude thumbnails by phase correlation, followed by grid-based local matching with normalized cross-correlation and subpixel refinement \cite{Lewis1995,GuizarSicairos2008}. A quadratic first-pass offset model and a linear residual second pass are then estimated with robust outlier rejection, and the final slave image is obtained by single-step resampling of the composed offsets. Among the candidate solutions, we retain the coregistration with the strongest coherence statistics.

Let slave image be $S_{\mathrm{coreg}}$ and master image $M$, the raw interferogram is formed by multiplying each master pixel with the complex conjugate of the corresponding slave pixel, so that their phase difference is encoded at pixel location $(r,c)$:
\begin{equation}
I(r,c)=M(r,c)\,\overline{S_{\mathrm{coreg}}(r,c)}.
\end{equation}

The local interferometric coherence is then estimated by the standard normalized complex correlation~\cite{Zebker1992,Hanssen2001}
\begin{equation}
\gamma(r,c)=
\frac{
\left|\left\langle M\,\overline{S_{\mathrm{coreg}}}\right\rangle_{W}\right|
}{
\sqrt{
\left\langle |M|^2 \right\rangle_{W}
\left\langle |S_{\mathrm{coreg}}|^2 \right\rangle_{W}
}
},
\label{eq:coherence}
\end{equation}
where $\langle \cdot \rangle_W$ denotes local averaging over a boxcar window. In our experiments, pairs with final mean coherence $\bar{\gamma}<0.3$ are rejected from downstream processing. For classical comparison, we retain both the raw wrapped interferogram and a Goldstein-filtered version \cite{Goldstein1998}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{I}_b=\mathcal{F}^{-1}\!\left(F_b\,H_b\right),
\qquad
H_b=\frac{|F_b|^{\alpha}}{\max\limits_{\mathbf{k}}\left(|F_b(\mathbf{k})|^{\alpha}\right)+\varepsilon},
\label{eq:goldstein}
\end{equation}

where $F_b=\mathcal{F}\{I_b\}$ is the Fourier transform of block $I_b$, $\hat{I}_b$ is the filtered block, and $H_b$ is a normalized spectral weighting mask proportional to $|F_b|^{\alpha}$. Here, $\alpha=0.5$ controls the filtering strength, $\varepsilon$ ensures numerical stability, and the filtered blocks are recombined by overlap-add weighting.

\subsection{Geometry-Conditioned FiLM-GPNet}

To model phase restoration across heterogeneous acquisition geometries, we propose a FiLM-conditioned U-Net model, called \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}. Figure \ref{FiLMUNet-Full} illustrates the overall architecture including sub-modules involved in the FiLM-GPNet. The network takes as input a three-channel interferometric stack such that $x_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times H \times W}$, where two out of the three channels correspond to the real and imagery parts of the raw interferogram, and third channel is the coherence map. Each pair is additionally associated with a normalized seven-dimensional geometry vector:
\begin{equation}
z_{ij} = [\Delta t_{ij}, \theta_{\mathrm{inc},ij}, \theta_{\mathrm{graze},ij}, B_{\perp,ij}, m_{ij}^{\mathrm{mode}}, m_{ij}^{\mathrm{look}}, \mathrm{SNR}_{ij}],
\end{equation}

where $\Delta t_{ij}$ is the temporal baseline, $\theta_{\mathrm{inc},ij}$ is the incidence angle, $\theta_{\mathrm{graze},ij}$ is the grazing angle, $B_{\perp,ij}$ is the perpendicular baseline, and the remaining entries encode acquisition mode, look direction, and a signal-to-noise proxy. The geometry vector is first passed through a separate two-layer MLP, which maps the normalized $7$-D input to a $64$-D conditioning code. This code is then injected into every encoder and decoder block through FiLM operation, which applies channel-wise affine modulation to intermediate feature maps:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{h} = \left(1+\gamma(z_{ij})\right)\odot h + \beta(z_{ij}),
\label{eq:film}
\end{equation}


where $h$ denotes the intermediate feature tensor, $\gamma(\cdot)$ and $\beta(\cdot)$ are learned channel-wise scale and shift parameters, and $\odot$ denotes elementwise multiplication. The backbone is a four-level U-Net with skip connections \cite{ronneberger2015u}, using channel widths $[32,64,128,256]$ and a 512-channel bottleneck. FiLM-conditioned double-convolution blocks are used throughout to suppress noise while preserving fringe structure. In each decoder stage, features are upsampled, concatenated with the corresponding skip connection, and refined by a FiLM-conditioned double-convolution block. The network has two output heads, one of which predicts a restored complex interferogram in two-channel real/imaginary form such that $\hat{y}_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times H \times W}$ and the other predicts a per-pixel log-variance map for aleatoric uncertainty estimation such that $\log \hat{\sigma}^{2}_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times H \times W}$.

\subsection{Pseudo-Supervised Training Objective}

Training is formulated as geometry-conditioned pseudo-supervised regression, with Goldstein-filtered interferograms used as pseudo-targets. The model thus learns a geometry-adaptive generalization of the classical Goldstein baseline without using reference DEMs or externally provided deformation labels. Although the supervisory signal comes from a classical interferometric filter, the network is designed to go beyond a fixed filter through geometry conditioning and uncertainty modeling. For each tile, FiLM-GPNet takes the raw interferogram and coherence channels as input and predicts a restored two-channel complex interferogram together with a per-pixel log-variance map. The total loss combines a wrapped-phase regression term, a heteroscedastic uncertainty loss, a fringe-preserving gradient loss, and, when valid triplet batches are available, a closure-consistency term computed from the predicted phases of interferometric triplets:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}
=
\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{unc}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{unc}}
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{cls}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cls}}
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{grd}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{grd}}.
\end{equation}

Here, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}$ matches the prediction to the Goldstein pseudo-target, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{unc}}$ models heteroscedastic uncertainty, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{grd}}$ preserves fringe gradients, and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cls}}$ enforces triplet closure. The scalar weights $\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}$, $\lambda_{\mathrm{unc}}$, $\lambda_{\mathrm{cls}}$, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{grd}}$ control their relative contributions. Overall, the objective defines a pseudo-supervised restoration model regularized by uncertainty and closure physics.


\begin{figure*}[t!]
  \centering
    \includegraphics[width=15cm]{Figures/main-results.png}
    \caption{Visual Comparison of intermittent outputs. Top row: baseline InSAR outputs, Bottom row: FiLM-GPNet derived outputs.}
    \label{main-results}
\end{figure*}


\begin{figure*}[t!]
  \centering
    \includegraphics[width=14cm]{Figures/DEM-Comp.png}
    \caption{Comparison of InSAR derived elevation profile: baseline vs FiLM-GPNet.}
    \label{DEM-Comp}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Uncertainty-Aware Downstream Processing}

After tile-wise inference and overlap-add reconstruction, the FiLM-GPNet-denoised interferogram is processed by a standard InSAR back-end: phase is unwrapped with SNAPHU, converted to elevation using the standard phase-to-height relation \cite{Hanssen2001}, and terrain-geocoded for comparison with external DEMs. These steps provide the end products used to assess whether geometry-conditioned phase restoration improves unwrapping stability and elevation recovery. Algorithm 1 in the appendix summarizes the complete workflow including geometry-aware prepossessing, classical InSAR baseline, FiLM-GPNet base phase denoising and underlying post processing tasks.



% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experiments and Results}

\subsection{Data and Experimental Setup}

The IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest dataset~\cite{ieeegrss2026dfc} comprises $791$ Capella Space X-band Spotlight SLCs across $39$ geographic AOIs. In this work, we use three stacks with distinct temporal and geometric characteristics: \textbf{AOI\_000 (Hawaii)}, \textbf{AOI\_024} (Westren Australia), and \textbf{AOI\_008} (Los Angeles). AOI\_000 is the densest stack, containing $221$ acquisitions acquired between June 2024 and November 2025, with predominantly descending but also some ascending passes and a broad incidence-angle range of $35.8^\circ$--$56.3^\circ$. By contrast, AOI\_024 contains $100$ acquisitions and is geometrically more homogeneous, with descending-only right-looking observations and incidence angles of $34.5^\circ$--$39.4^\circ$, while AOI\_008 contains $119$ acquisitions, is ascending-only and right-looking, and spans a longer temporal interval with incidence angles of $35.3^\circ$--$41.4^\circ$. This combination provides a dense geometry-diverse stack for primary evaluation together with two additional stacks for controlled cross-stack and zero-shot assessment. After strict pair-graph filtering, we retain geometrically compatible pairs and valid closure triplets within each AOI for training and evaluation. Training uses an AOI-aware temporal split by acquisition date to avoid leakage across nearby observations, and all metrics are reported on the processed test stacks using $256\times256$ tiles with stride $128$.


The IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest dataset~\cite{ieeegrss2026dfc} comprises $791$ Capella Space X-band raw spotlight SLCs across $39$ different geographic AOIs. The primary evaluation site is \textbf{AOI\_000 (Hawaii)}: 221 collects, ascending and descending orbits, incidence angles $35.8^\circ$--$56.3^\circ$, covering active volcanic and forested terrain on the Big Island. After pair-graph filtering, 8{,}834 candidate pairs and 24{,}171 strict triplets are available; we process the 224 highest-scoring short-temporal-baseline pairs ($\Delta t \le 6$\,days). Training uses an AOI-based 70/15/15\,\% temporal split (by collect date) to prevent geographic leakage; all metrics are reported on the full processed set. Tile generation yields ${\approx}150{,}000$ training tiles at $256\!\times\!256$ pixels.

Training details are as follows: AdamW (${\rm lr}=10^{-4}$, weight decay $10^{-5}$), cosine annealing with 2-epoch warm-up, batch size 8, 50 epochs on a single NVIDIA~A100 (40\,GB). Tile size $256\!\times\!256$, stride 128, yielding ${\sim}150{,}000$ training tiles. Seed 42. Inference runs at ${\approx}3$\,s/pair on CPU or ${\approx}0.2$\,s/pair on GPU, scaling to the full 791-SLC contest dataset in under 15\,min.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

\textcolor{blue}{For quantitative evaluation, we use four metrics that assess pairwise consistency, downstream usability, elevation quality, and stack-level temporal stability. These metrics include \emph{Triplet Closure Error}, \emph{Unwrap Success Rate}, \emph{DEM NMAD} and \emph{Temporal Residual}. The definitions of these metrics along with their mathematical equations are discussed in the supplementary material.}


\subsection{Results}

Table~\ref{tab:metrics} reports our four-metric evaluation protocol applied to 224 processed pair pertaining to the geographic sub-stack of Hawaii. It can be seen from the table that our proposed FiLM-GPNet based phase denoising, surpasses the classical Goldstein filter on all metrics. Specifically, in terms of temporal consistency residual, FiLM-GPNet reduces the unweighted SBAS stack residual from 1.158\,rad to 0.367\,rad with a ($68.3\%$) improvement. This large gain reflects how geometry conditioning makes the denoised stack far more self-consistent.
The triplet closure error also improves by 10.1\%. The marked disparity between these two metrics is itself an insight: geometry-aware denoising primarily benefits \emph{stack-level} temporal coherence, not only pairwise consistency.
The unwrap success rate is nearly identical for both methods with (${\approx}26\%$ connected coverage), confirming FiLM-GPNet introduces no artifacts that degrade phase unwrapping. Low absolute coverage reflects the fundamentally limited coherence of X-band spotlight returns over Hawaii's dense tropical canopy and volcanic rock, which is a scene-level constraint.
Similarly, DEM NMAD shows a slight $-1.7\%$ improvement (40.1\,m $\to$ 39.4\,m). The large absolute NMAD reflects the large height-of-ambiguity ($h_{\rm amb}\!\approx\!100$--$400$\,m for $|B_\perp|\!=\!20$--$50$\,m at X-band), which amplifies residual phase noise in the coherent valid pixels. Fig.~\ref{fig:dem_comparison} shows the Copernicus GLO-30 reference DEM and the InSAR-derived height maps for the most sensitive pair ($|B_\perp|\!=\!780$\,m, $h_{\rm amb}\!=\!12$\,m); the elevation histogram (top right) provides the valid cross-system comparison.
Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal_residual} visualises the per-pair temporal residual reduction across the stack.

