\subsection{Uncertainty-Aware Downstream Processing}

After tile-wise inference and overlap-add reconstruction, the FiLM-GPNet-denoised interferogram is processed by a standard InSAR back-end: phase is unwrapped with SNAPHU, converted to elevation using the standard phase-to-height relation \cite{Hanssen2001}, and terrain-geocoded for comparison with external DEMs. These steps provide the end products used to assess whether geometry-conditioned phase restoration improves unwrapping stability and elevation recovery. Algorithm 1 in the appendix summarizes the complete workflow including geometry-aware prepossessing, classical InSAR baseline, FiLM-GPNet base phase denoising and underlying post processing tasks.



% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experiments and Results}

\subsection{Data and Experimental Setup}

The IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest dataset~\cite{ieeegrss2026dfc} comprises $791$ Capella Space X-band Spotlight SLCs across $39$ geographic AOIs. In this work, we use three stacks with distinct temporal and geometric characteristics: \textbf{AOI\_000 (Hawaii)}, \textbf{AOI\_024} (Westren Australia), and \textbf{AOI\_008} (Los Angeles). AOI\_000 is the densest stack, containing $221$ acquisitions acquired between June 2024 and November 2025, with predominantly descending but also some ascending passes and a broad incidence-angle range of $35.8^\circ$--$56.3^\circ$. By contrast, AOI\_024 contains $100$ acquisitions and is geometrically more homogeneous, with descending-only right-looking observations and incidence angles of $34.5^\circ$--$39.4^\circ$, while AOI\_008 contains $119$ acquisitions, is ascending-only and right-looking, and spans a longer temporal interval with incidence angles of $35.3^\circ$--$41.4^\circ$. This combination provides a dense geometry-diverse stack for primary evaluation together with two additional stacks for controlled cross-stack and zero-shot assessment. After strict pair-graph filtering, we retain geometrically compatible pairs and valid closure triplets within each AOI for training and evaluation. Training uses an AOI-aware temporal split by acquisition date to avoid leakage across nearby observations, and all metrics are reported on the processed test stacks using $256\times256$ tiles with stride $128$.


The IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest dataset~\cite{ieeegrss2026dfc} comprises $791$ Capella Space X-band raw spotlight SLCs across $39$ different geographic AOIs. The primary evaluation site is \textbf{AOI\_000 (Hawaii)}: 221 collects, ascending and descending orbits, incidence angles $35.8^\circ$--$56.3^\circ$, covering active volcanic and forested terrain on the Big Island. After pair-graph filtering, 8{,}834 candidate pairs and 24{,}171 strict triplets are available; we process the 224 highest-scoring short-temporal-baseline pairs ($\Delta t \le 6$\,days). Training uses an AOI-based 70/15/15\,\% temporal split (by collect date) to prevent geographic leakage; all metrics are reported on the full processed set. Tile generation yields ${\approx}150{,}000$ training tiles at $256\!\times\!256$ pixels.

Training details are as follows: AdamW (${\rm lr}=10^{-4}$, weight decay $10^{-5}$), cosine annealing with 2-epoch warm-up, batch size 8, 50 epochs on a single NVIDIA~A100 (40\,GB). Tile size $256\!\times\!256$, stride 128, yielding ${\sim}150{,}000$ training tiles. Seed 42. Inference runs at ${\approx}3$\,s/pair on CPU or ${\approx}0.2$\,s/pair on GPU, scaling to the full 791-SLC contest dataset in under 15\,min.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

\textcolor{blue}{For quantitative evaluation, we use four metrics that assess pairwise consistency, downstream usability, elevation quality, and stack-level temporal stability. These metrics include \emph{Triplet Closure Error}, \emph{Unwrap Success Rate}, \emph{DEM NMAD} and \emph{Temporal Residual}. The definitions of these metrics along with their mathematical equations are discussed in the supplementary material.}


\subsection{Results}

Table~\ref{tab:metrics} reports our four-metric evaluation protocol applied to 224 processed pair pertaining to the geographic sub-stack of Hawaii. It can be seen from the table that our proposed FiLM-GPNet based phase denoising, surpasses the classical Goldstein filter on all metrics. Specifically, in terms of temporal consistency residual, FiLM-GPNet reduces the unweighted SBAS stack residual from 1.158\,rad to 0.367\,rad with a ($68.3\%$) improvement. This large gain reflects how geometry conditioning makes the denoised stack far more self-consistent.
The triplet closure error also improves by 10.1\%. The marked disparity between these two metrics is itself an insight: geometry-aware denoising primarily benefits \emph{stack-level} temporal coherence, not only pairwise consistency.
The unwrap success rate is nearly identical for both methods with (${\approx}26\%$ connected coverage), confirming FiLM-GPNet introduces no artifacts that degrade phase unwrapping. Low absolute coverage reflects the fundamentally limited coherence of X-band spotlight returns over Hawaii's dense tropical canopy and volcanic rock, which is a scene-level constraint.
Similarly, DEM NMAD shows a slight $-1.7\%$ improvement (40.1\,m $\to$ 39.4\,m). The large absolute NMAD reflects the large height-of-ambiguity ($h_{\rm amb}\!\approx\!100$--$400$\,m for $|B_\perp|\!=\!20$--$50$\,m at X-band), which amplifies residual phase noise in the coherent valid pixels. Fig.~\ref{fig:dem_comparison} shows the Copernicus GLO-30 reference DEM and the InSAR-derived height maps for the most sensitive pair ($|B_\perp|\!=\!780$\,m, $h_{\rm amb}\!=\!12$\,m); the elevation histogram (top right) provides the valid cross-system comparison.
Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal_residual} visualises the per-pair temporal residual reduction across the stack.

