\begin{abstract}
Dense temporal stacks of commercial X-band SAR imagery offer fine-grained surface-deformation monitoring. Yet phase quality degrades sharply when acquisitions span wide ranges of incidence angles and orbital geometries, which is a common scenario in multi-orbit Capella Space constellation stacks.  Classical Goldstein filtering ignores this geometry diversity, yielding inconsistent interferograms and elevated triplet-closure errors.  This paper proposes \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}, a FiLM-conditioned U-Net that denoises wrapped complex interferograms while conditioning on a seven-dimensional geometry vector $[\Delta t,\,\theta_{\rm inc},\,\theta_{\rm graze},\,B_\perp,\,{\rm mode},\,{\rm look},\,{\rm SNR}]$.  Training is entirely self-supervised: a Noise2Noise objective on frequency-domain sub-look splits is augmented with triplet-closure consistency and SBAS temporal residual losses where no clean reference interferograms are required. The model additionally predicts per-pixel aleatoric uncertainty, which propagates downstream as weights in SBAS stack inversion.  Evaluated on the 221-collect Hawaii stack from the IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest (AOI\_000), FiLM-GPNet reduces triplet closure error by 10.1\% (1.018$\to$0.915\,rad) and temporal stack residual by \textbf{68.3\%} (1.158$\to$0.367\,rad) over Goldstein, with the full pipeline reproducible from public Capella Space data.

Github Repo link: \href{https://github.com/getnetdemil/Learning-Assisted-InSAR-DEM-Enhancement}{https://github.com/getnetdemil/Learning-Assisted-InSAR-DEM-Enhancement}
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
InSAR, phase denoising, pseudo-supervised learning, FiLM conditioning,
SBAS, zero-shot generalization, Capella Space, phase closure
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

Dense temporal stacks from commercial X-band SAR constellations expose a fundamental limitation of classical InSAR: geometry-blind filters such as Goldstein apply uniform smoothing regardless of acquisition geometry, producing inconsistent interferograms, elevated triplet-closure errors, and unreliable SBAS inversion across heterogeneous stacks~\cite{goldstein1998interferometric,11186241}. Supervised deep learning is constrained by the absence of clean reference interferograms in operational settings~\cite{massonnet1998radar,zebker1992decorrelation}.

We propose \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}, a geometry-conditioned pseudo-supervised phase restoration network. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A FiLM-conditioned U-Net that adapts phase restoration to per-pair acquisition geometry (temporal baseline, incidence angle, $B_\perp$, look direction, SNR) without clean reference labels.
  \item A pseudo-supervised training objective using Goldstein outputs as proxy targets, augmented with triplet-closure and SBAS temporal residual losses to enforce physics consistency.
  \item Per-pixel aleatoric uncertainty prediction, propagated as SBAS inversion weights for uncertainty-aware downstream processing.
  \item Zero-shot generalization: a model trained on one geographic stack transfers without retraining to a geographically and geometrically distinct site.
\end{itemize}
% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Method}
Figure 1 in the appendix provides an overview of the various steps involved in the overall data processing pipeline. These are discussed individually in the following sub-sections.

\subsection{Geometry-Aware Pair-Graph Construction}

\begin{figure*}[t!]
  \centering
    \includegraphics[width=16cm]{Figures/FiLMUNet-Full.png}
    \caption{The FiLM-GPNet architecture. It comprises  input/output blocks, FiLM conditioning-based encoder/decoder network and MLP for processing of geometry feature vector.}
    \label{FiLMUNet-Full}
\end{figure*}


Given a manifest of $N$ raw SLC acquisitions within each spatial group, we construct a strict interferometric pair graph that retains only geometrically compatible pairs for InSAR processing. An edge $(i,j)$ is accepted only if the two acquisitions belong to the same AOI, share orbit state, look direction, orbital plane, platform, and instrument mode, and satisfy temporal and geometric consistency constraints. In our experiments, we require $\Delta t \leq 30$ days and $|\Delta \theta_{\mathrm{inc}}| \leq 2^\circ$, while additionally enforcing bounded look-angle and squint-angle differences when available. We also reject pairs with excessive range or azimuth sampling mismatch and require sufficient footprint overlap through a lightweight bounding-box test. This conservative filtering removes weak or physically inconsistent pairs before coregistration and interferogram formation. The surviving edges are ranked by a soft quality score $Q_{ij}$ as defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:qscore}
\begin{equation}
Q_{ij}
=
\frac{1}{1+\Delta t_{ij}}
\cdot
\frac{1}{1+|\Delta \theta_{\mathrm{inc},ij}|}
\cdot
G_{ij},
\label{eq:qscore}
\end{equation}
where $G_{ij}$ is an additional geometry-consistency factor that penalizes look-angle mismatch, squint mismatch, range/azimuth sampling mismatch, and low footprint overlap whenever those metadata are available. Closure triplets are then enumerated from the accepted graph for later physics-based supervision.

\subsection{PFA-Aware Coregistration and Classical Baseline}

Because the Capella Spotlight PFA SLC pairs used in this work can exhibit geometry-dependent misregistration and variable interferometric quality, we apply a compact engineering pipeline for precise pairwise alignment before learning-based phase restoration. For each accepted pair, a metadata-derived scene-reference seed is refined on multilooked log-amplitude thumbnails by phase correlation, followed by grid-based local matching with normalized cross-correlation and subpixel refinement \cite{Lewis1995,GuizarSicairos2008}. A quadratic first-pass offset model and a linear residual second pass are then estimated with robust outlier rejection, and the final slave image is obtained by single-step resampling of the composed offsets. Among the candidate solutions, we retain the coregistration with the strongest coherence statistics.

Let slave image be $S_{\mathrm{coreg}}$ and master image $M$, the raw interferogram is formed by multiplying each master pixel with the complex conjugate of the corresponding slave pixel, so that their phase difference is encoded at pixel location $(r,c)$:
\begin{equation}
I(r,c)=M(r,c)\,\overline{S_{\mathrm{coreg}}(r,c)}.
\end{equation}

The local interferometric coherence is then estimated by the standard normalized complex correlation~\cite{Zebker1992,Hanssen2001}
\begin{equation}
\gamma(r,c)=
\frac{
\left|\left\langle M\,\overline{S_{\mathrm{coreg}}}\right\rangle_{W}\right|
}{
\sqrt{
\left\langle |M|^2 \right\rangle_{W}
\left\langle |S_{\mathrm{coreg}}|^2 \right\rangle_{W}
}
},
\label{eq:coherence}
\end{equation}
where $\langle \cdot \rangle_W$ denotes local averaging over a boxcar window. In our experiments, pairs with final mean coherence $\bar{\gamma}<0.3$ are rejected from downstream processing. For classical comparison, we retain both the raw wrapped interferogram and a Goldstein-filtered version \cite{Goldstein1998}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{I}_b=\mathcal{F}^{-1}\!\left(F_b\,H_b\right),
\qquad
H_b=\frac{|F_b|^{\alpha}}{\max\limits_{\mathbf{k}}\left(|F_b(\mathbf{k})|^{\alpha}\right)+\varepsilon},
\label{eq:goldstein}
\end{equation}

where $F_b=\mathcal{F}\{I_b\}$ is the Fourier transform of block $I_b$, $\hat{I}_b$ is the filtered block, and $H_b$ is a normalized spectral weighting mask proportional to $|F_b|^{\alpha}$. Here, $\alpha=0.5$ controls the filtering strength, $\varepsilon$ ensures numerical stability, and the filtered blocks are recombined by overlap-add weighting.

\subsection{Geometry-Conditioned FiLM-GPNet}

To model phase restoration across heterogeneous acquisition geometries, we propose a FiLM-conditioned U-Net model, called \textbf{FiLM-GPNet}. Figure \ref{FiLMUNet-Full} illustrates the overall architecture including sub-modules involved in the FiLM-GPNet. The network takes as input a three-channel interferometric stack such that $x_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times H \times W}$, where two out of the three channels correspond to the real and imagery parts of the raw interferogram, and third channel is the coherence map. Each pair is additionally associated with a normalized seven-dimensional geometry vector:
\begin{equation}
z_{ij} = [\Delta t_{ij}, \theta_{\mathrm{inc},ij}, \theta_{\mathrm{graze},ij}, B_{\perp,ij}, m_{ij}^{\mathrm{mode}}, m_{ij}^{\mathrm{look}}, \mathrm{SNR}_{ij}],
\end{equation}

where $\Delta t_{ij}$ is the temporal baseline, $\theta_{\mathrm{inc},ij}$ is the incidence angle, $\theta_{\mathrm{graze},ij}$ is the grazing angle, $B_{\perp,ij}$ is the perpendicular baseline, and the remaining entries encode acquisition mode, look direction, and a signal-to-noise proxy. The geometry vector is first passed through a separate two-layer MLP, which maps the normalized $7$-D input to a $64$-D conditioning code. This code is then injected into every encoder and decoder block through FiLM operation, which applies channel-wise affine modulation to intermediate feature maps:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{h} = \left(1+\gamma(z_{ij})\right)\odot h + \beta(z_{ij}),
\label{eq:film}
\end{equation}


where $h$ denotes the intermediate feature tensor, $\gamma(\cdot)$ and $\beta(\cdot)$ are learned channel-wise scale and shift parameters, and $\odot$ denotes elementwise multiplication. The backbone is a four-level U-Net with skip connections \cite{ronneberger2015u}, using channel widths $[32,64,128,256]$ and a 512-channel bottleneck. FiLM-conditioned double-convolution blocks are used throughout to suppress noise while preserving fringe structure. In each decoder stage, features are upsampled, concatenated with the corresponding skip connection, and refined by a FiLM-conditioned double-convolution block. The network has two output heads, one of which predicts a restored complex interferogram in two-channel real/imaginary form such that $\hat{y}_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times H \times W}$ and the other predicts a per-pixel log-variance map for aleatoric uncertainty estimation such that $\log \hat{\sigma}^{2}_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times H \times W}$.

\subsection{Pseudo-Supervised Training Objective}

Training is formulated as geometry-conditioned pseudo-supervised regression, with Goldstein-filtered interferograms used as pseudo-targets. The model thus learns a geometry-adaptive generalization of the classical Goldstein baseline without using reference DEMs or externally provided deformation labels. Although the supervisory signal comes from a classical interferometric filter, the network is designed to go beyond a fixed filter through geometry conditioning and uncertainty modeling. For each tile, FiLM-GPNet takes the raw interferogram and coherence channels as input and predicts a restored two-channel complex interferogram together with a per-pixel log-variance map. The total loss combines a wrapped-phase regression term, a heteroscedastic uncertainty loss, a fringe-preserving gradient loss, and, when valid triplet batches are available, a closure-consistency term computed from the predicted phases of interferometric triplets:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}
=
\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{unc}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{unc}}
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{cls}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cls}}
+
\lambda_{\mathrm{grd}} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{grd}}.
\end{equation}

Here, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}$ matches the prediction to the Goldstein pseudo-target, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{unc}}$ models heteroscedastic uncertainty, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{grd}}$ preserves fringe gradients, and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{cls}}$ enforces triplet closure. The scalar weights $\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}$, $\lambda_{\mathrm{unc}}$, $\lambda_{\mathrm{cls}}$, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{grd}}$ control their relative contributions. Overall, the objective defines a pseudo-supervised restoration model regularized by uncertainty and closure physics.


